<script is:inline type="application/ld+json">{`{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My mortar joints are crumbling. How urgent is it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Urgent — every Chicago winter makes it worse. Freeze-thaw forces water into failing joints, expanding them with every cycle. Tuckpointing now costs 3–5× less than the structural repair you'll need after two more winters. If you can scrape mortar out with a key, call today."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"There's white powder on my brick. Should I be worried?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — that's efflorescence, and it's a water migration signal, not just a stain. Mineral salts reach the surface because water is moving through your masonry. We find the source, fix it, then address the staining — so it doesn't return in six months."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"I see cracks forming between my brick and mortar. What does that mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It means the bond has failed and water has a direct path inside. In Chicagoland, that crack will be 3× wider by spring. Repointing now seals it before the next freeze-thaw cycle turns a $400 repair into a $3,000 wall rebuild."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What exactly is tuckpointing and do I actually need it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tuckpointing means removing deteriorated mortar and packing fresh mortar to spec — it's the single most important maintenance job on any masonry structure. If your mortar is more than 25 years old in Chicagoland, it's likely failing whether you can see it or not."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"How much does tuckpointing cost in Chicago?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tuckpointing in Chicago typically runs $8–$25 per linear foot depending on access, joint depth, and mortar condition. Full facade work on a 3-flat runs $3,000–$12,000. We provide written estimates after a free on-site assessment — we don't quote over photos."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"How long does tuckpointing last in Chicago's climate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Done correctly with matched mortar composition, tuckpointing holds 20–30 years in Chicagoland. Done incorrectly — wrong mortar hardness, wrong joint profile — it fails in 3–5 years and damages the surrounding brick. We match original composition and profile every time."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"One brick fell out. Can I just replace that one brick?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Possibly — but a single failed brick almost always signals a larger moisture or settling pattern nearby. We assess the surrounding structure before touching anything, then match the brick color, size, and mortar blend so the repair is invisible."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My brick is spalling (surface flaking off). Is it structural?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Spalling is a freeze-thaw problem and it escalates fast. Once the face of a brick breaks away, water enters the porous core and the damage accelerates through every winter cycle. Early spalling repair is cosmetic. Late-stage spalling becomes a full wythe replacement — 10× the cost."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Can you match old brick that's no longer manufactured?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — we source salvage brick and regional suppliers who carry historic grades. On 1920s–1960s homes across Oak Park, Hinsdale, and the North Shore, we've matched dozens of discontinued profiles. The repair should be invisible, not a patch job."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My chimney cap is cracked. How serious is that?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Very serious — a cracked chimney cap is the #1 water entry point for your flue. Most homeowners ignore it because it looks minor from the ground. A $400 cap repair now prevents a $6,000–$10,000 chimney rebuild in 2–3 years."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a full chimney rebuild or just repairs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually repairs — we give you an honest scope, not the most expensive option. Crown repair, flashing replacement, and tuckpointing the chimney stack covers most cases. Full rebuilds above the roofline are needed when bricks have shifted, the liner is compromised, or water damage has penetrated the structure."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"When is the best time to repair a chimney in Chicago?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Late summer through fall — before the freeze-thaw season begins. Mortar cures best above 40°F and needs time to fully set before the first freeze. Spring is also good if you missed fall. Never delay into winter — one freeze-thaw cycle in a compromised chimney can push a repair into a rebuild."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know if my masonry damage is structural or cosmetic?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If cracks run diagonally, walls bow outward, or mortar is missing in multiple joints in the same area — it's structural. Hairline cracks in a single location are usually cosmetic. We assess both and tell you honestly which category you're in — no upselling."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Will my insurance cover masonry repair?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes — sudden damage from storms, falling objects, or vehicle impact is typically covered. Gradual deterioration from deferred maintenance is not. We provide detailed written documentation of damage scope and cause that you can submit to your insurer directly."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My concrete driveway has cracks. Repair or replace?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Depends on the crack pattern. Hairline cracks under 1/4 inch wide with no vertical displacement — repair and seal. Wide cracks, significant heaving, or crumbling edges — replacement is more cost-effective long-term. We give you an honest assessment, not a default recommendation to replace everything."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Can you repair natural stone without replacing it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In most cases, yes. Stone is far more durable than the mortar around it — most stone masonry repair is repointing failed joints, not replacing the stone itself. When stone is cracked or spalled, we match the original material and profile as closely as possible."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"I have a limestone facade. What do I need to know?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Limestone is common on Chicago and North Shore homes built between 1890 and 1950. It's soft, it stains, and it reacts badly to Portland-heavy mortar. We use lime-based mortars that flex with the stone instead of locking against it — the wrong mortar accelerates limestone deterioration."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Is power washing safe for brick and masonry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the wrong pressure, no — high PSI strips the face from soft brick and drives water into joints. We use low-pressure soft washing for masonry — typically under 500 PSI — which removes dirt, algae, and staining without damaging the surface or compromising mortar joints."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Do you work on commercial buildings and multi-family properties?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — we work on 2-flats through mid-rise commercial buildings. We're experienced with occupied buildings, working around business hours, and coordinating with property managers. Our crew handles commercial scope without subcontracting any part of the work."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Why does historic masonry require different techniques?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pre-1950 masonry used softer brick and lime-based mortar designed to flex and breathe. Modern Portland cement mortar is harder than historic brick — it locks the wall rigid, cracks the surrounding brick, and traps moisture inside. We use period-appropriate materials because the wrong repair causes more damage than the original problem."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My limestone steps are crumbling at the edges. What's happening?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Edge spalling on limestone steps is a freeze-thaw problem — water infiltrates the porous stone, freezes, and breaks away the face. Once started, it accelerates with every winter. Early repair is a consolidation and fill. Late-stage means step replacement. Don't wait another season."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What is a lintel and how do I know if mine is failing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A lintel is the structural beam — steel, stone, or concrete — that spans your window and door openings and carries the masonry above. Failing lintels show as diagonal cracks radiating from the corners of openings, or bricks that are visibly dropping or separating above windows. This is structural — don't wait."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My parapet wall is leaning. How serious is that?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Extremely serious — a leaning parapet is a falling masonry risk. Parapet walls on Chicago flat-roof buildings are exposed to freeze-thaw from both sides and are often the first masonry on a building to fail. A leaning parapet requires immediate assessment. This is a life-safety issue."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My retaining wall is bulging outward. What's causing it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Hydrostatic pressure — water building up behind the wall with nowhere to drain. Bulging means the wall is approaching failure. Left unaddressed, it will collapse. We assess drainage first, then determine whether the wall can be repaired or needs to be rebuilt with proper drainage installed behind it."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"My brick paver driveway is sinking in sections. What's the fix?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sunken pavers are almost always a base failure — the compacted gravel base has settled or washed out. The pavers come up, base is re-graded and recompacted, and pavers go back down. If the pavers themselves are intact, this is relatively straightforward and far cheaper than full replacement."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Should I waterproof my brick exterior?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the product and your specific situation. Breathable silane/siloxane sealers are appropriate for most Chicagoland brick — they repel water while allowing vapor to escape. Film-forming sealers trap moisture inside and accelerate spalling. Never let a contractor apply a film sealer to your historic brick."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"I see cracks in my foundation wall. How serious is it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Depends on the crack type. Hairline vertical cracks are usually shrinkage — low concern. Horizontal cracks in block foundations indicate lateral pressure — serious, act immediately. Stair-step cracks in brick foundations indicate differential settlement — assess urgently."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"There's a growing gap between my front steps and the house. What's happening?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The stoop is settling away from the foundation — this is extremely common on Chicago homes. The steps are on their own footing, which moves independently from the house foundation. The gap opens every year. Left unaddressed, it becomes a water entry point directly against your foundation wall."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What free tools does All Masonry Services offer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Three tools built specifically for Illinois homeowners: (1) Chimney Risk Score — 12-point structural checklist, 2 minutes, tells you low/medium/high risk. (2) Brick Damage Counter — count damaged bricks, get a repair estimate with urgency rating. (3) Brick Project Estimator — material and labor ranges for new brick work. All free, no login, PDF download included."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Do you use subcontractors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Never. Every job is completed by our own crew — from Naperville to Winnetka. No handoffs, no third parties on your property, no surprise faces on job day. That's why we have zero callbacks on record across 500+ completed projects since 2007."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a quote?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Call 773-656-2107 or submit the contact form. We schedule a free on-site assessment and hand you a written estimate — no obligation. We don't quote over photos. Masonry is structural and a quote from a photo almost always leads to surprises on job day. We'd rather get it right."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What warranty do you provide?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If anything we've done fails within the warranty period, we return and fix it at no charge. No debate, no invoice. Specific terms vary by scope and are provided in writing with every estimate before you sign anything."}}
  ]
}`}</script>

